export default function SmartCoolerPage() {
  return (
    <PageTemplate
      title="Smart Cooler: Scalable Edge AI for Retail Cooler Networks"
      image={heroImage}
      metadata={[
        { label: 'Role', value: 'Full-stack AI engineer in a team of three' },
        { label: 'Stack', value: 'React, FastAPI, ONNX, Edge Impulse, Redis, RubikPi3' },
        { label: 'Context', value: 'Qualcomm internship, client-facing demo' },
        { label: 'Outcome', value: 'Fully on-device; adding a cooler needs no frontend changes' }
      ]}
    >
      <p>
        A dual-camera edge AI system for retail coolers built to run entirely on a Qualcomm QCS6490-powered RubikPi3 and scale horizontally to an arbitrary fleet of refrigerators without modifying a line of frontend code. One camera profiles the age and gender of shoppers approaching the cooler; a second tracks which products are taken or restocked from the shelves. Both inference streams feed a React dashboard that surfaces restocking recommendations, demographic trends, and product-level demand signals.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every inference step runs on the device itself, with models quantized and optimized for the QCS6490 SoC. No cloud API calls, no external services. A single-cooler deployment is entirely self-contained; a multi-cooler fleet scales through a thin Redis-backed aggregation hub with zero changes to the per-device code or the frontend.
      </p>

      <h2>Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        Each cooler unit runs two parallel inference pipelines as isolated subprocesses managed by a FastAPI application serving their event streams over the local network. When multiple coolers are deployed, each device pushes events into a shared Redis Stream keyed by <code>cooler_id</code>. A stateless hub service aggregates those streams and exposes the same API surface the frontend already expects, so the dashboard needs no changes to switch from a single unit to a fleet of any size.
      </p>

      <ArchitectureDiagram />

      <p>
        The design is deliberately asymmetric: Redis and the hub live on a central server; the devices remain stateless and interchangeable. If a unit goes offline, its events stop arriving but the rest of the fleet continues unaffected. Provisioning a new cooler requires syncing the backend code to a new Pi and running the device setup script. Nothing else changes anywhere in the system.
      </p>

      <h2>On-Device Model Deployment</h2>
      <p>
        Both models were quantized and optimized to run efficiently on the QCS6490's CPU and neural processing hardware. The demographics model is exported to ONNX and executed via ONNX Runtime, which handles op-level graph optimizations including constant folding, node fusion, and memory layout transformations at session initialization time. The can detector is packaged through the Edge Impulse pipeline as a <code>.eim</code> binary that wraps the trained object detection model with a hardware-aware runtime optimized for Arm-class processors.
      </p>
      <p>
        Keeping inference fully on-device eliminates round-trip latency to any cloud endpoint and makes the system functional in air-gapped retail environments. The QCS6490 was selected specifically for its balance of computer vision throughput and power envelope at the edge.
      </p>

      <h3>INT8 Quantization and the NPU</h3>
      <p>
        The team quantized both models for the QCS6490: the public demographics model with Qualcomm's tools, and the can detector through Edge Impulse. To show what INT8 inference on the chip's neural processing unit buys, this is the YOLO11 benchmark I ran on the same chip for another Qualcomm demo, against the CPU:
      </p>
      <table style={{margin: '20px 0', borderCollapse: 'collapse', width: '100%'}}>
        <thead>
          <tr style={{borderBottom: '2px solid #333'}}>
            <th style={{padding: '10px', textAlign: 'left'}}>Metric</th>
            <th style={{padding: '10px', textAlign: 'right'}}>CPU</th>
            <th style={{padding: '10px', textAlign: 'right'}}>NPU, INT8</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr style={{borderBottom: '1px solid #ddd'}}>
            <td style={{padding: '10px'}}>Inference time per frame</td>
            <td style={{padding: '10px', textAlign: 'right'}}>304 ms</td>
            <td style={{padding: '10px', textAlign: 'right'}}><strong>38 ms (8x faster)</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr style={{borderBottom: '1px solid #ddd'}}>
            <td style={{padding: '10px'}}>Frames per second</td>
            <td style={{padding: '10px', textAlign: 'right'}}>3.3</td>
            <td style={{padding: '10px', textAlign: 'right'}}><strong>26.9</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr style={{borderBottom: '1px solid #ddd'}}>
            <td style={{padding: '10px'}}>Memory</td>
            <td style={{padding: '10px', textAlign: 'right'}}>~870 MB</td>
            <td style={{padding: '10px', textAlign: 'right'}}>~870 MB</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        On this hardware, quantization is what turns a few frames a second into real-time video on a small, low-power board.
      </p>

      <h2>Demographics Inference Pipeline</h2>
      <p>
        The outside camera runs a two-stage inference pipeline per captured frame. Stage one is a ResNet-10 SSD face detector executed via OpenCV DNN (Caffe model), applying non-maximum suppression to resolve overlapping detections. Each face crop is padded by 10% and resized to 224×224 before being passed to stage two.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stage two is an ONNX Runtime session running <code>age_gender_modern.onnx</code>, a model with ImageNet-normalized inputs that outputs a continuous age scalar and a gender probability. The pipeline applies temporal smoothing over a rolling window to suppress per-frame classification noise: gender is determined by majority vote across the last several predictions within a bounded time window. When the window detects a gap indicating a new person has approached, it resets rather than inheriting the prior person's vote history. Smoothed predictions are serialized as JSON to stdout and consumed by the event collector.
      </p>

      <div style={{margin: '30px 0', textAlign: 'center'}}>
        <img src={demographicsDetection} alt="Face detected and annotated with age and gender" style={{maxWidth: '100%', height: 'auto', borderRadius: '8px', boxShadow: '0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1)'}} />
        <p style={{marginTop: '10px', fontStyle: 'italic', color: '#666', fontSize: '0.9em'}}>
          Each detected face is annotated with gender and age group. The annotated frame is saved to disk and its path included in the JSON event payload.
        </p>
      </div>

      <pre style={{background: 'var(--code-bg, #f5f5f5)', padding: '16px', borderRadius: '8px', overflowX: 'auto', fontSize: '0.85em'}}>
{`{
  "timestamp": "2026-05-25T14:30:00+00:00",
  "source": "demographics",
  "cooler_id": "cooler-store-a",
  "image_url": "http://192.168.1.153:8000/captures/demo_20260525_ab12cd.jpg",
  "detections": [
    { "gender": "Male", "gender_confidence": 94.2, "age_group": "25-32" }
  ]
}`}
      </pre>

      <h2>Can Detection Pipeline</h2>
      <p>
        The inside camera runs an object detection model packaged by Edge Impulse as a <code>.eim</code> binary, executed through the Edge Impulse Linux SDK's <code>ImageImpulseRunner</code>. The model was trained on labeled images of the specific cooler's product SKUs and outputs per-class bounding-box detections with confidence scores.
      </p>
      <p>
        Raw detections are fed through an IoU-based multi-object tracker before being emitted as events. A bounding box must appear consistently across multiple consecutive frames before it is considered a confirmed detection, and its reported position is averaged across those frames to suppress jitter from camera vibration or minor occlusion. Detections below the confidence gate are discarded before reaching the tracker. After smoothing, confirmed detections are saved as annotated JPEGs at a configurable rate and emitted as structured JSON events. The dashboard infers product state transitions (taken vs. restocked) by watching when a product class appears or disappears across the event stream.
      </p>

      <div style={{margin: '30px 0', textAlign: 'center'}}>
        <img src={detectionsInFridge} alt="Bounding boxes drawn on products inside the cooler" style={{maxWidth: '100%', height: 'auto', borderRadius: '8px', boxShadow: '0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1)'}} />
        <p style={{marginTop: '10px', fontStyle: 'italic', color: '#666', fontSize: '0.9em'}}>
          The can detector draws bounding boxes around each product class with label and confidence score, after IoU-based tracking stabilizes the detections.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Event Bus and Multi-Cooler Aggregation</h2>
      <p>
        <code>collector.py</code> manages both inference subprocesses and acts as the per-device event bus. It reads JSON lines from each subprocess's stdout in dedicated threads and appends to an in-memory circular buffer. When <code>REDIS_URL</code> is set, it additionally pushes each event to a shared Redis Stream via <code>redis_store.push_event()</code>. Redis failures are caught and logged without interrupting the local collection loop, so a network partition degrades gracefully to local-only storage.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each event pushed to Redis carries the device's <code>cooler_id</code> and <code>cooler_ip</code>, allowing the hub to construct image URLs pointing back to the originating device. The stream is capped to prevent unbounded growth. The hub reads from that stream and re-exposes the same endpoint contract the frontend already uses, adding per-cooler filtering and a fleet enumeration endpoint. The frontend is unaware of whether it is talking to a single Pi or a hub backed by a dozen devices.
      </p>

      <h2>The Dashboard</h2>
      <p>
        The React frontend polls a single configurable URL, either the hub for a fleet or a Pi directly for a standalone unit. All analytics are derived client-side from the raw event stream; the backend serves events and images only.
      </p>

      <div style={{margin: '30px 0', textAlign: 'center'}}>
        <img src={dashboard1} alt="Smart Cooler dashboard: KPI tiles, product activity, and live event feed" style={{maxWidth: '100%', height: 'auto', borderRadius: '8px', boxShadow: '0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1)'}} />
        <p style={{marginTop: '10px', fontStyle: 'italic', color: '#666', fontSize: '0.9em'}}>
          KPI tiles, product activity table, and the live combined event feed from all cameras.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>KPI tiles</strong>: shoppers today, peak hour, products taken today, top-demand product this week.</li>
        <li><strong>Product Activity table</strong>: per-SKU taken / restocked / net counts with a color-coded demand bar.</li>
        <li><strong>Ins and Outs over time</strong>: grouped bar chart of takes and restocks per product, switchable between today's hourly view and the current week.</li>
        <li><strong>Demographics by Product</strong>: correlates can detections with shopper detections within a 3-minute window to show gender split and age group per SKU.</li>
        <li><strong>Weekly trends</strong>: total shopper traffic and product activity over the current week.</li>
        <li><strong>Live event feed</strong>: real-time interleaved stream from all cameras across the fleet.</li>
        <li><strong>Recent captures</strong>: thumbnail grids of the latest annotated frames from each camera.</li>
      </ul>

      <div style={{margin: '30px 0', textAlign: 'center'}}>
        <img src={dashboard2} alt="Demographics-by-product correlation panel and weekly trends" style={{maxWidth: '100%', height: 'auto', borderRadius: '8px', boxShadow: '0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1)'}} />
        <p style={{marginTop: '10px', fontStyle: 'italic', color: '#666', fontSize: '0.9em'}}>
          The demographics-by-product panel correlates shopper profiles to specific SKUs using a 3-minute time-window join on the event stream.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Scalability Design</h2>
      <p>
        The core scalability property of the system is that each layer is independently stateless. Every Pi runs inference, buffers events locally, and optionally mirrors to Redis. The hub reads from that stream and builds responses on demand without maintaining per-device state. The frontend polls one endpoint and renders whatever it receives. Adding a cooler to the fleet means provisioning a new Pi with the same backend code. No shared infrastructure changes, no schema migrations, no frontend deployments.
      </p>
      <p>
        In standalone mode there is no Redis dependency at all: the Pi's FastAPI server is the only backend, and the frontend points directly at it. Migrating from standalone to fleet mode requires setting the Redis URL on each Pi and pointing the frontend at the hub. No code changes in either case.
      </p>
      <p>
        The subprocess architecture keeps each camera pipeline fault-isolated. A crash in the can detector does not affect the demographics stream or the API layer. The subprocess's stderr is forwarded to the API process log for diagnostics without contaminating the structured JSON event stream on stdout.
      </p>
    </PageTemplate>
  );
}
